Let $E_1$ be the number of eggs Chester eats in 30 days when he eats 3 eggs per day.
$E_1 = 3 \text{ eggs/day} \times 30 \text{ days} = 90 \text{ eggs}$

Now, Chester increases his egg consumption to 5 eggs per day for 30 days.
Let $E_2$ be the number of eggs Chester eats in 30 days when he eats 5 eggs per day.
$E_2 = 5 \text{ eggs/day} \times 30 \text{ days} = 150 \text{ eggs}$

However, we are given that Chester increases his consumption to 5 eggs per day.
Let $E_3$ be the number of eggs Chester eats in 30 days when he eats 5 eggs per day.
$E_3 = 5 \text{ eggs/day} \times 30 \text{ days} = 150 \text{ eggs}$
We want to determine how many dozens of eggs Chester needs for 60 days.

First, let's consider the initial 30 days. Chester eats 3 eggs per day, so in 30 days, he eats $3 \times 30 = 90$ eggs.
Then for the next 30 days, Chester eats 5 eggs per day, so in 30 days, he eats $5 \times 30 = 150$ eggs.
In total, for 60 days, Chester eats $90 + 150 = 240$ eggs.

Now, we want to find how many dozens of eggs Chester needs. Since there are 12 eggs in a dozen, we divide the total number of eggs by 12.
Number of dozens = $\frac{240}{12} = 20$ dozens.

We are given that Chester eats 3 eggs a day for 30 days.
Total eggs for 30 days = $3 \times 30 = 90$ eggs.
Then he increases to 5 eggs a day for 30 days.
